A POSITIVE CONTROL CAUGHT A FALSE PREMISE BEFORE THE PRODUCER CHANGED. The code
said the catalogue "registers both `::` and `.` forms", so re-spelling looked
free. It is not: `IoPrimitive.qualified_name` joins module and name with a DOT
unconditionally, so a `module: std::env` row is indexed as the MIXED
`std::env.consts` plus a dotted alias and NEVER as `std::env::consts`.
Emitting the language's own spelling would have silently lost every rust and
C++ attribute classification. The qualified index now folds separators on the
read side -- through ONE shared helper, because the same gap reappeared inside
the fix: folding in `lookup` but not in `lookup_with_module` left
`classify_call` still returning None.

